    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required that when a newly computed `customerCode` collides with an existing owner's, a `-<n>` suffix (smallest `n \u2265 2`) be appended to make it unique.\n\n**Change** in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (the owner-create flow):\n\n1. Replaced the direct assignment at line 208 with a de-duplicating call:\n   ```java\n   owner.setCustomerCode(deduplicateCustomerCode(owner.buildCustomerCode()));\n   ```\n\n2. Added a `deduplicateCustomerCode(String)` helper that collects all existing owners' non-null `customerCode`s (via `clinicService.findAllOwners()`, matching the pattern used by the sibling `requireUniqueIdentity`/`flagPossibleDuplicate` methods), returns the computed code unchanged if free, and otherwise appends `-2`, `-3`, \u2026 choosing the smallest `n \u2265 2` that yields an unused code.\n\n3. Added the needed imports (`java.util.Objects`, `java.util.stream.Collectors`).\n\n**Verification:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the `AcceptanceTest.coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` test, which asserts distinct owners always get distinct customer codes, passes).\n\nA note on the test design, echoed in its own comment: a true hash collision can't be forced black-box because identical `telephone + lastName` is already blocked by telephone-uniqueness, so the test asserts the rule's guarantee (distinct codes) rather than exercising the suffix path directly.",
